    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine when Wanda, Darren, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the math lab, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their work schedules. Wanda works every 4 days, Darren works every 3 days, Beatrice works every 6 days, and Chi works every 7 days. The LCM will give us the number of days after which all four of them will be in the lab at the same time.\n\nWe'll start by finding the LCM of each pair of work schedules, and then we'll extend that to the three pairs (Wanda with Darren, Darren with Beatrice, and Beatrice with Chi). Finally, we'll find the smallest number that appears in all these LCMs, which will be the LCM of the three pairs and also the LCM of the LCMs of the pairs.\n\n1. First, calculate the LCM of 4 and 3:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(4, 3) = 12 \\]\n2. Next, calculate the LCM of 12 and 6:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(12, 6) = 12 \\]\n3. Then, calculate the LCM of 12 and 7:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(12, 7) = 84 \\]\n\nSo, the least common multiple of their work schedules is 84. This means they will all be in the lab together every 84 school days.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{84}\\).\n```python\nimport math\nfrom functools import reduce\n\n# Function to compute LCM of two numbers\ndef lcm(a, b):\n    return abs(a * b) // math.gcd(a, b)\n\n# Function to compute LCM of a list of numbers\ndef lcm_multiple(numbers):\n    return reduce(lcm, numbers)\n\n# Work schedules in days\nwork_schedules = [4, 3, 6, 7]\n\n# Find the LCM of the work schedules\nlcm_work_schedules = lcm_multiple(work_schedules)\nprint(lcm_work_schedules)\n```",
